@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@command{apt-man} is a comprehensive command-line tool for managing APT
package sources, including Ubuntu PPAs and third-party repositories, along
with their GPG signing keys. It provides an intuitive interface for common
tasks that would otherwise require manual file editing and complex APT
commands.

@section Features

@itemize @bullet
@item
List and categorize all APT sources
@item
Enable and disable sources without deleting them
@item
Show packages available in or installed from specific sources
@item
Bulk upgrade sources between Ubuntu releases
@item
Comprehensive GPG key management and validation
@item
Support for both legacy .list and modern .sources formats
@item
Identify security issues like missing or expired keys
@end itemize

@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

@section Installation

Copy @file{apt-man.sh} to a directory in your PATH:

@example
sudo cp apt-man.sh /usr/local/bin/apt-man
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/apt-man
@end example

@section Basic Usage

List all sources:
@example
apt-man list
@end example

List sources with their signing keys:
@example
apt-man list --keys
@end example

Run comprehensive security audit:
@example
apt-man lint
@end example

Check for key problems:
@example
apt-man keys --check
@end example

@node Source Management
@chapter Source Management

@section Listing Sources

@deffn Command list [@option{--keys}]
List all APT sources, including both active and disabled ones. Sources are
numbered for easy reference in other commands. If @option{--keys} is specified,
also shows the GPG signing key for each source.

Active sources are shown normally, while disabled sources are marked with
@code{[disabled]} in the output.
@end deffn

@section Enabling and Disabling Sources

@deffn Command disable @var{id}
Disable a source by renaming it with a @file{.disabled} extension. The source
remains on the system but is not used by APT. This is safer than deleting
sources as they can be easily re-enabled.

Cannot be used on the main @file{/etc/apt/sources.list} file.
@end deffn

@deffn Command enable @var{id}
Enable a previously disabled source by removing the @file{.disabled} extension.
The source becomes active immediately but requires @command{apt update} to
refresh package lists.
@end deffn

@deffn Command list-disabled
Show only disabled sources. This is useful for reviewing what sources are
available but not currently active.
@end deffn

@section Package Operations

@deffn Command show @var{id}
Show all packages available in the source identified by @var{id}. This queries
the repository's package index directly.
@end deffn

@deffn Command installed @var{id}
Show all packages currently installed on your system that came from the source
identified by @var{id}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command remove @var{id}
Remove all packages installed from the source identified by @var{id}. This is
useful before removing a third-party repository to avoid leaving orphaned
packages.

@strong{Warning:} This operation removes packages without confirmation. Use with
caution.
@end deffn

@section Security Audit

@deffn Command lint
Run comprehensive security audit of all sources and keys. Performs 17 different
checks including:
@itemize @bullet
@item Deprecated keyring files
@item Legacy .list file formats
@item Sources without explicit keys
@item Missing or unreadable key files
@item Keys in legacy locations
@item Expired or expiring keys
@item HTTP sources (should use HTTPS)
@item Weak key algorithms
@item Duplicate sources
@item Unreachable URLs
@item End-of-Life releases
@item File permissions and ownership
@item Missing security repositories
@item Development/testing repositories
@item Unnecessary deb-src entries
@item Architecture mismatches
@end itemize
@end deffn

@deffn Command lint @option{--fix}
Interactively fix security warnings found by the audit. This command provides
a safe, interactive way to apply fixes:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Scans for HTTP sources and tests if HTTPS is available before converting
@item
Identifies legacy keys in @file{trusted.gpg.d/} that should be moved
@item
Detects unreachable sources and offers to disable or remove them
@item
Prompts for confirmation before each change
@item
Creates timestamped backup directory for all modified files
@item
Tests URL reachability before making changes
@item
Provides clear instructions for required manual steps
@item
Shows summary of applied, skipped, and failed fixes
@end itemize

@strong{Unreachable Sources:}
When an unreachable source is detected, you can choose to:
@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{d)} Disable the source (rename to .disabled) - safe, reversible
@item @strong{r)} Remove the source file completely - permanent deletion with backup
@item @strong{s)} Skip and leave as is
@end itemize

@strong{Safety Features:}
@itemize @bullet
@item All changes require explicit confirmation
@item Backups are created before any modifications
@item HTTPS URLs are tested before conversion
@item Instructions provided for complex operations
@item No changes to main @file{sources.list}
@item Removal requires double confirmation
@end itemize

Example:
@example
sudo apt-man lint --fix
@end example
@end deffn

@section Security Fixes

@deffn Command migrate @var{id}
Convert a legacy .list file to modern .sources (DEB822) format. Currently provides
manual migration steps. The modern format is more structured and supports explicit
key references via the Signed-By field.

@strong{Note:} This feature currently guides you through manual migration steps.
Automatic migration is planned for a future release.
@end deffn

@deffn Command use-https @var{id}
Convert an HTTP source to HTTPS for improved security. The command:
@itemize @bullet
@item Tests URL reachability before making changes
@item Creates a backup of the original file
@item Updates all HTTP URLs in the file to HTTPS
@item Provides instructions for testing with @command{apt update}
@end itemize

@strong{Note:} Some repositories may not support HTTPS. The command will fail
gracefully if the HTTPS URL is not reachable.
@end deffn

@section Upgrading Sources

@deffn Command upgrade-source @var{old} @var{new}
Upgrade all APT sources from @var{old} release codename to @var{new} release
codename. This modifies all source files to reference the new release.

For example, to upgrade from Ubuntu Noble to Oracular:
@example
apt-man upgrade-source noble oracular
@end example

After upgrading sources, run:
@example
sudo apt update
sudo apt dist-upgrade
@end example
@end deffn

@node Key Management
@chapter Key Management

@section Listing Keys

@deffn Command keys [@option{--list}]
List all GPG keys on the system used for package verification. Keys are
categorized by their format and location, helping identify outdated key
storage methods. This is the default action when using @command{keys}
without any options.
@end deffn

@section Checking Keys

@deffn Command keys @option{--check}
Validate all sources for missing or problematic GPG keys. This command checks:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Whether referenced key files exist
@item
Whether key files are readable
@item
Sources without explicit keys (potential security risk)
@item
Key format (old vs new)
@end itemize

Reports any issues found with actionable information.
@end deffn

@section Refreshing Keys

@deffn Command keys @option{--refresh}
Refresh GPG keys from keyservers to get updates, revocations, or new
signatures. This only affects keys in @file{/etc/apt/keyrings/}.

Keys in @file{/usr/share/keyrings/} are managed by APT packages and should
be updated via @command{apt upgrade}.
@end deffn

@section Key Information

@deffn Command keys @option{--info} @var{keyfile}
Show detailed information about a specific GPG key file, including:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Key ID
@item
Algorithm and key strength
@item
Creation date
@item
Expiration date (if any)
@item
Whether the key is expired
@item
Key UIDs (identities)
@end itemize

Example:
@example
apt-man keys --info /etc/apt/keyrings/microsoft.gpg
@end example
@end deffn

@section Moving Legacy Keys

@deffn Command keys @option{--move} @var{keyfile}
Move a legacy key from @file{/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/} to the modern location
@file{/etc/apt/keyrings/}. This command:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Validates the key is in the legacy location
@item
Creates @file{/etc/apt/keyrings/} if it doesn't exist
@item
Copies the key to the new location
@item
Sets appropriate file permissions
@item
Provides instructions for updating source files
@end itemize

@strong{Important:} After moving a key, you must update your .sources files
to explicitly reference the new key location using the @code{Signed-By} field.
Test with @command{apt update} before removing the old key file.

Example:
@example
apt-man keys --move /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/old.gpg
@end example
@end deffn

@section Key Renewal Planning

@deffn Command keys @option{--renewal}
Show all GPG keys that will expire in the next 90 days. This helps plan for
key renewals before they cause APT update failures. For each expiring key,
displays:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Key file path
@item
Key ID
@item
Expiration date
@item
Days remaining until expiration
@end itemize

Regular use of this command helps maintain system security and avoid surprise
APT failures due to expired keys.
@end deffn

@node Key Formats
@chapter Understanding Key Formats

@section Legacy Formats (Deprecated)

@subsection OLD (deprecated)
The single-file @file{/etc/apt/trusted.gpg} keyring. This format is deprecated
and should not be used for new keys. All keys in this file are automatically
trusted by APT.

@subsection OLD (trusted.gpg.d)
Keys stored in @file{/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/*.gpg}. While better than the
single-file approach, this is still an older format. Keys here are
automatically trusted by APT without explicit source references.

@section Modern Formats (Recommended)

@subsection NEW (apt keyring)
Keys in @file{/etc/apt/keyrings/*.gpg}. This is the recommended location for
user-managed keys. Sources must explicitly reference these keys using the
@code{Signed-By} field in DEB822 format or equivalent options.

This approach provides better security through explicit trust relationships.

@subsection NEW (system keyring)
Keys in @file{/usr/share/keyrings/*.gpg}. These are installed and updated
via APT packages (e.g., @code{ubuntu-keyring}). Generally used for
distribution-provided keys.

@section Special Formats

@subsection INLINE (embedded key)
PGP keys embedded directly in DEB822 @file{.sources} files using the
@code{Signed-By} field. These cannot be refreshed from keyservers but are
self-contained within the source definition.

@node Source Formats
@chapter Understanding Source Formats

@section Legacy Format (.list files)

The traditional one-line-per-source format:
@example
deb http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu noble main restricted
deb-src http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu noble main restricted
@end example

Limitations:
@itemize @bullet
@item
No explicit key references
@item
Uses default keyring only
@item
Less structured
@end itemize

@section Modern Format (.sources files)

The structured DEB822 format introduced in Ubuntu 22.04+:
@example
Types: deb
URIs: http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
Suites: noble
Components: main restricted universe multiverse
Signed-By: /usr/share/keyrings/ubuntu-archive-keyring.gpg
@end example

Advantages:
@itemize @bullet
@item
Explicit key references via @code{Signed-By}
@item
Support for inline PGP keys
@item
More structured and extensible
@item
Better security through explicit trust
@end itemize

@node Examples
@chapter Common Usage Examples

@section Example 1: Listing All Sources

@example
$ apt-man list
Detected APT sources:
--------------------------------------
[01] 3rd Party / PPA      https://packages.microsoft.com/...
[02] 3rd Party / PPA      https://packages.mozilla.org/apt
[03] Ubuntu Official      http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
[04] Ubuntu Official      http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
--------------------------------------
@end example

@section Example 2: Viewing Source Keys

@example
$ apt-man list --keys
APT sources with their signing keys:
--------------------------------------
[01] 3rd Party / PPA      https://packages.microsoft.com/...
     Key: NEW (apt keyring)  /etc/apt/keyrings/microsoft.gpg
[02] Ubuntu Official      http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
     Key: NEW (system keyring)  /usr/share/keyrings/ubuntu-...
--------------------------------------
@end example

@section Example 3: Temporarily Disabling a PPA

@example
$ apt-man list
[02] 3rd Party / PPA      https://ppa.launchpad.net/...

$ apt-man disable 2
Disabling source: /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ppa.sources
Source disabled. Run 'sudo apt update' to apply changes.

$ apt-man list
[02] 3rd Party / PPA      [disabled] https://ppa.launchpad.net/...
@end example

@section Example 4: Checking for Key Problems

@example
$ apt-man keys --check
Checking for missing or problematic keys...
--------------------------------------
[OK] NEW (apt keyring): https://packages.microsoft.com/...
     Key: /etc/apt/keyrings/microsoft.gpg

[ERROR] Missing key file: /etc/apt/keyrings/missing.gpg
        Required by: https://example.com/repo
        File: /etc/apt/sources.list.d/example.sources
--------------------------------------
Found issues with one or more keys.
@end example

@section Example 5: Upgrading Ubuntu Release

@example
$ apt-man upgrade-source noble oracular
Upgrading all APT sources from noble to oracular ...
Upgrade complete. You may now run: sudo apt update

$ sudo apt update
$ sudo apt dist-upgrade
@end example

@node Security
@chapter Security Considerations

@section Key Verification

Always verify GPG keys before adding them to your system:

@enumerate
@item
Download the key from the official source
@item
Verify the key fingerprint matches published values
@item
Check the key's UIDs and signatures
@item
Store in @file{/etc/apt/keyrings/} with appropriate permissions
@end enumerate

@section Best Practices

@itemize @bullet
@item
Use explicit @code{Signed-By} references in @file{.sources} files
@item
Prefer keys in @file{/etc/apt/keyrings/} over @file{/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/}
@item
Regularly run @command{apt-man --check-keys} to identify issues
@item
Keep keys updated, especially for third-party repositories
@item
Remove unused sources and their keys
@item
Be cautious with sources that don't specify keys
@item
Review key expiration dates periodically
@end itemize

@section Common Security Issues

@subsection Missing Keys
Sources without keys or with missing key files can be compromised. Always
ensure keys are present and valid.

@subsection Expired Keys
Expired keys may indicate abandoned repositories or security issues. Check
with @command{apt-man --key-info}.

@subsection Legacy Key Formats
Keys in @file{/etc/apt/trusted.gpg} or @file{/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/} are
automatically trusted. Migrate to explicit references when possible.

@node Files
@chapter Important Files and Directories

@table @file
@item /etc/apt/sources.list
Main APT sources file (may not exist on modern systems).

@item /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.list
Legacy format source files.

@item /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources
Modern DEB822 format source files.

@item /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.disabled
Disabled source files (both formats).

@item /etc/apt/trusted.gpg
Deprecated keyring file. Avoid using.

@item /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/*.gpg
Legacy keyring directory. Still functional but old format.

@item /etc/apt/keyrings/*.gpg
Modern user-managed keyring directory (recommended).

@item /usr/share/keyrings/*.gpg
System-managed keyring directory (package-managed).

@item /tmp/sources_index
Temporary index file created during source operations.
@end table

